data.content:
I have enough information. Here's what happened:

---

**Root cause: C# 14 span overload resolution change**

In C# 14 (.NET 10), new built-in `Span<T>` / `ReadOnlySpan<T>` conversions mean `array.Contains(num)` now binds to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(Span<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

`MemoryExtensions.Contains` is **not representable in a LINQ expression tree**, so the compiled expression throws at runtime (expression trees can only call methods on `IEnumerable<T>`, not span-based extensions).

**Fix — force binding to `Enumerable.Contains`:**

```csharp
// Option 1: Cast to IEnumerable<int>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 2: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 3: Call Enumerable.Contains explicitly
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Any of these ensures the expression tree references the `Enumerable.Contains` method, which the expression interpreter can handle.
